            <ArticleTitle>[Salmonella enterica serovar typhimurium infection presenting as a retrocecal mass in an 8-year-old child].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1562-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.arcped.2010.08.015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Minor salmonellosis is due to Gram-negative bacilli, which usually cause enterocolitis with potentially severe complications. We report on a case of a clinically uncommon presentation of Salmonella enterica serovar typhimurium infection in an 8-year-old child who presented with acute abdominal pain. We discuss clinically uncommon presentations of salmonella disease in children, as well as its pathology and radiology.</AbstractText>
